\section{Which area code the grid is keyed on}{

The parquet is rasterized from present-day polygons through
\code{inst/extdata/regions.csv}, so its \code{area_code} is a raw reporting-area code
and \strong{not} necessarily a \link{polity_area_crosswalk} \code{polity_area_code}: the
bucket every polity-keyed national table in whep is aggregated on. Grid
codes that are not a bucket cannot join to national data at all: the join
is silently empty on both sides.

\code{area_key} selects which of the two the output carries. It is not a
fallback: \code{"grid"} is the default, reproduces the parquet's own codes
bit-for-bit, and warns naming the codes that cannot resolve;
\code{"polity_area"} resolves each code to its bucket through
\link{polity_area_crosswalk} and re-sums \code{polity_frac} within
\verb{(lon, lat, area_code)}, so a cell straddling two areas of the same bucket
stays one row per bucket and each cell's fractions still sum to 1. It
respects \code{options(whep.unfold_rest_of_world = TRUE)} (see
\code{\link[=folded_reporting_areas]{folded_reporting_areas()}}), so the grid and the national tables agree
about where a Rest-of-World member's rows belong.

Under \code{"polity_area"} the raw reporting code is \strong{carried, not replaced}:
the output gains \code{grid_area_code} holding the parquet's own code, joined with
\code{+} where a cell's areas collapse into one bucket. So the fold this performs
is recoverable at the join rather than baked into the grid — a derived key
silently overwriting the raw one it came from is what whep#582 reports from
the output side, and the same fold is what dropped Sudan's 40.8 M goats and
doubled its sugar cane in the published production series (whep#563).

The output deliberately does \strong{not} gain \code{polity_code} /
\verb{reporting_polity_*}. A bucket is not a polity: \code{999} holds up to 17
territories at once and \code{206} holds Sudan and South Sudan together, so no
polity code string is recoverable from this year-less grid. Carrying that
identity needs the cell x polity x validity-interval unit tracked by epic
whep#458, not a column added here.
}

\section{Where the table comes from}{

WHEP produces this crosswalk itself, in section 1b of
\code{inst/scripts/prepare_spatialize_all.R}, from Natural Earth polygons and
\code{inst/extdata/regions.csv}. It is therefore published as the
\code{spatialize-cell-polity-fraction} pin alongside the nine other artefacts of
that script, and the pin is what this function reads by default: no user has
to run the producer, or hold the Natural Earth shapefile, to get the table
WHEP's own runs use. \code{WHEP_POLITY_FRACTION_PATH} and \code{polity_fraction_path}
are \strong{overrides} for a local development build, in the shape
\code{\link[=read_polycell_support]{read_polycell_support()}} already uses.
}

\section{Vintage of the area vocabulary}{

The table's \code{area_code} values must all exist in the \code{regions.csv} the
installed package carries, because that is the table its producer
rasterizes through. A copy built through an older vintage keyed Ethiopia
\code{62} and Sudan (former) \code{206} where today's lookup uses \code{238} and \code{276}, so
adopting it deleted both countries from every consumer (whep#694). Such a
table is \strong{refused} with class \code{whep_stale_cell_polity_grid} rather than
read, and the message names the producer re-run that rebuilds it. The check
guards the override, which is the only route that can now go stale, but it
runs on the pin too so that pinning an older \code{version} cannot reintroduce
the deletion either.
}

\section{Year-aware support}{

With \code{year} set, the support is not this crosswalk at all: it is the
polycell support (\code{\link[=read_polycell_support]{read_polycell_support()}}) read at the validity interval
covering \code{year}, so each cell carries the polity the grid holds that year.
In 1961 the former-USSR cells carry the USSR (\code{228}), Czechoslovakia's carry
\code{51} and Yugoslavia's \code{248}; FAOSTAT reports their national totals under
those codes, which the year-invariant crosswalk has no cell for (whep#1196).
Each code is resolved from \code{polity_code} through \link{polity_area_crosswalk},
and, under \code{area_key = "polity_area"}, folded onto its bucket.

Where the polity grid and FAOSTAT's reporting units disagree, a recorded
mapping (\code{inst/extdata/polity_cell_support_map.csv}) keys the cells, and
every mapped row is labelled in \code{polity_rule}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"aggregate_member"}: an aggregate reported by FAOSTAT whose members the
grid carries separately takes the union of its members' cells in its
reporting years (Belgium-Luxembourg \code{15} to 1999, Viet Nam \code{237} to 1974,
Yemen \code{249} in 1961).
\item \code{"contained_fold"}: a polity with no FAOSTAT row in the year folds into
the reporting unit containing it (the Baltic and Azerbaijan SSRs into the
USSR to 1990, the fifteen USSR successors into it in 1991, North
Macedonia into \code{248} in 1991).
\item \code{"constant_territory"}: Yemen \code{249} in 1962-1989, for which the grid
carries no complete predecessor, is placed on modern Yemen's cells.
}

Some cells carry an aggregate over its members, or two claimants, so their
polities' territory adds up to more than the cell. In such an overlapping
cell, a polity with no area code, none of \code{reporting_areas}, or duplicating
the container it folds into, is removed from the share denominator. The
removed polycells are returned in the \code{"deduplicated"} attribute, and the
overlapping cells where two reporting polities still share are returned in
the \code{"overlap_kept"} attribute.

\code{polity_frac} is then the polity's share of the cell's measured \strong{land},
the same basis as the carbon path's \code{cell_area_frac} (which is carried too,
with the same value), rather than the crosswalk's subcell count. The land of
a polity with no area code stays in the denominator, so it is never handed
to a neighbour. \code{cell_area_ha} keeps the latitude formula of the
year-invariant path.
}

